\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Reflection positivity of a massive scalar lattice}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On the nearest-neighbor torus of \ref{fdip-007M}, choose either
reflection #{\theta}. Give every undirected edge a nonnegative
weight #{w_e} with #{w_{\theta e}=w_e}, and fix #{m>0}.
The real scalar law
##{\begin{aligned}
H(\phi)&=\frac12\sum_{e=\{u,v\}}w_e(\phi_u-\phi_v)^2
       +\frac{m^2}{2}\sum_{v\in X}\phi_v^2,\\
d\mu(\phi)&=Z^{-1}e^{-H(\phi)}
 \prod_{v\in X}d\phi_v
\end{aligned}}
is reflection invariant and reflection positive on
#{\mathcal A_+} and its positive-time #{L^2} domain.
Every polynomial in the positive-half field coordinates belongs
to that domain. This is the [massive scalar precision](fdip-000A)
with zero-weight edges omitted. It uses ordinary differences,
equivalently trivial transport in the
[connection energy](fdip-006D).}

\proof{
\p{Since #{H(\phi)\ge(m^2/2)\|\phi\|^2}, the normalizer is finite
and positive, and every polynomial moment exists. Reflection is a
permutation of the coordinates, preserves Lebesgue measure and
preserves #{H} by the weight hypothesis. This proves invariance
separately from positivity.}

\p{For a site reflection, no nearest-neighbor edge joins
#{X_+\setminus X_0} directly to #{X_-\setminus X_0}.
Partition the terms of #{H} into #{H_+}, those supported in
#{X_+} but not wholly in #{X_0}; their reflected partners
#{H_-=\Theta H_+}; and #{H_0}, those supported wholly in
#{X_0}. Include onsite mass terms in the same partition.
Because reflection fixes every boundary coordinate,
##{-H=A+\Theta A,\qquad A=-H_+-\tfrac12H_0.}
In particular the boundary terms are counted once.}

\p{For a link reflection the halves are disjoint. The only
crossing edges are the time bonds at the two cuts, and each has
the form #{e=\{v,\theta v\}} with a unique #{v\in X_+}.
Its contribution to #{-H} is
##{-\frac{w_e}{2}\phi_v^2
   -\frac{w_e}{2}\phi_{\theta v}^2
   +w_e\phi_v\phi_{\theta v}.}
Put its first term into #{A}, its second into #{\Theta A}, and
write the last as #{C_e\Theta C_e}, where
#{C_e=\sqrt{w_e}\phi_v}. All within-half edge energies and
onsite mass terms are assigned to their own half, with a minus
sign. Reflection invariance of the weights then gives the
[positive-half decomposition](fdip-007N).}

\p{For #{R>0}, restrict every coordinate to #{[-R,R]} and use the
uniform probability on this interval as the product reference.
On that cube the functions #{A,C_e} are bounded, so the lemma
proves reflection positivity of the normalized cutoff law
#{\mu_R}. The constant reference-density factor cancels from
normalization; thus #{\mu_R} is exactly #{\mu} conditioned on
#{\max_v|\phi_v|\le R}. For bounded #{f,g}, dominated convergence
in the integrable Gaussian density gives
##{B_{\mu_R}(f,g)\longrightarrow B_\mu(f,g).}
For polynomials the same argument uses a polynomial times the
dominating massive Gaussian. In particular positivity survives
the limit. Finally, bounded value truncations are dense in
#{\mathcal H_+}; the norm bound in \ref{fdip-007M} extends
the pairing and its positivity to all of that domain.}
}

\p{Compare \citet{pp. 443–444}{friedli2017statistical},
Examples 10.9–10.10, for the half decompositions.
The cutoff argument above supplies the noncompact Gaussian step.
Arbitrary inhomogeneous weights need not respect the chosen
reflection. Arbitrary fixed connections need not have the displayed
nonnegative crossing terms. Neither case is asserted by this theorem.}
